@@ -0,0 +1,91 @@
+# Export acceptance and recovery bug hunt
+
+Checked revision: `1775be1df6db105015bc744fd61a4567eee62f50`.
+Scope: `Queue.submit`, `Queue.work`, constructor recovery, and result persistence,
+against `app/export-contract.md:8–19`. Stopped at this operation and its recovery
+path. Product code, governing records, and existing evidence are unchanged.
+
+## Finding: recovered accepted jobs can become permanently stranded on retry
+
+**High severity — accepted work never completes after a transient storage failure.**
+
+Locations: `app/export_queue.py:28–40`, especially the committed running update
+at lines 34–35 and the pending-only selection at lines 29–30. Recovery at
+lines 17–20 runs automatically only during construction (line 15).
+
+Reproduction with `python3 tests/hunt_witness.py`:
+
+1. Submit job `job` with payload `payload`; acceptance returns after commit.
+2. A child worker commits `running`, then terminates via `os._exit(73)` at the
+   supported after-claim boundary. No output exists.
+3. After the child exits, open a replacement worker; recovery restores pending.
+4. After its next running commit, acquire a SQLite writer lock through a separate
+   connection. The hook returns normally. Production result insertion raises
+   `sqlite3.OperationalError: database is locked`.
+5. Release the lock and repeatedly call `work()` on the replacement worker.
+   All five calls return `False`; state remains `running`, result remains `None`.
+
+The extra connection supplies transient storage contention, not another worker
+or overlapping Queue request. There is exactly one worker process at a time;
+the first process has exited before replacement. No corruption or storage loss
+is involved, and the job ID always has the same payload.
+
+The output transaction rolls back, but the earlier running transaction remains
+committed. No active execution owns that running job after the exception. The
+selection predicate excludes it indefinitely, so the five retries demonstrate
+a stable stuck state rather than merely a slow finite retry schedule. Another
+restart or an explicit `recover()` would rescue it, but the supported contract
+promises repeated work calls after transient failure; it does not require an
+additional process restart after each such failure. A similar failure before
+the running commit does not have this mechanism because pending is retained.
+
+Expected: after storage recovers, retries eventually persist the original
+payload and mark the job done. Observed witness exit: **1**, with:
+
+```text
+PASS: interrupted accepted job completes after restart
+After lock release: attempts=[False, False, False, False, False], state=running, result=None
+AssertionError: accepted job stranded after restart and transient I/O failure
+```
+
+Repair direction (not implemented): ensure a failed output attempt leaves work
+eligible for retry, including when an attempted cleanup itself encounters I/O
+failure. Preserve the atomic result/done transaction and restart recovery.
+
+## Coverage and limits
+
+Executable coverage in `tests/hunt_witness.py` uses real on-disk SQLite files in
+a temporary directory, a real child-process exit, and real SQLite lock failure;
+no product SQL is mocked. Its control confirms that an accepted job interrupted
+after the running commit completes on restart, returns the original payload,
+and subsequently needs no work. It checks the persisted running state and
+absence of output before replacement. The failure scenario exercises the same
+restart followed by a transient output failure and repeated calls after release.
+The script exits nonzero on violated assertions and zero when its checks hold.
+
+Code inspection additionally covered:
+
+- Acceptance: insertion commits before the accepted response. Interruption
+  before commit cannot have returned acceptance; after commit the row survives.
+- Pending at restart: constructor recovery leaves pending jobs eligible.
+- Interrupted running work: constructor resets running to pending before work.
+- Output boundary: result insertion and done update share one transaction;
+  process interruption cannot durably split them under the stated SQLite
+  contract. A committed done row is not selected again, including after restart.
+- Repeated submissions with the same ID and payload preserve the existing job.
+  Changing the payload for an existing ID is outside the supplied contract and
+  was not reported as a bug.
+
+Record discovery read the root architecture and gate, the single-writer claim
+as context, and the note-payload specification and import requirement to check
+applicability. References to the queue and its stable identifiers revealed no
+additional governing record or external consumer. Claim proof and verification
+files were not used or changed; this is a contract bug hunt, not a new claim
+verification verdict. All test data stays local and outside project `data/`.
+
+This was targeted execution plus code inspection, not exhaustive fault
+injection. Disk-full, commit-time I/O errors, every SQLite statement boundary,
+and interruption inside SQLite internals were not dynamically enumerated.
+Concurrent workers, overlapping Queue requests, changing job payloads, storage
+loss, and corruption are excluded by the supported contract. Note handling,
+import policy, and unrelated application operations were not investigated.
